Guile-Plotutils NEWS - History of user-visible changes

Changes in 1.1.0 (since 1.0.3)

* New features

** Error bars.  graph's new #:x-error and #:y-error options draw error
   bars.  Each takes a number or a (lower . upper) pair for every point,
   a list with one bar (or #f for none) per point, or a procedure of x
   and y.  The new bounds->errors procedure converts absolute limits,
   such as confidence intervals, into error amounts.  Bars are clipped
   to the plotting area, and axis limits computed from the data include
   them.

** Per-dataset styles.  The new dataset procedure makes a set of data
   with its own linemode, symbol, line width, fill fraction, color,
   symbol font, and error bars; pass graph a list of them:
     (graph (list (dataset xs ys) (dataset xs zs #:symbol 3)))
   graph's options are the defaults for every dataset.

** Several graphs on one page.  The new multigraph and subgraph
   procedures, and graph's new #:reposition option, place graphs on one
   page, as GNU graph's --reposition option does, e.g. for insets.

** Font listing.  The new list-fonts procedure in (plotutils plot)
   returns the fonts an output format, or a plotter, supports.

** (plotutils plot) has new procedures deletepl!, to delete a plotter
   at once rather than when it is garbage collected (Postscript and CGM
   output is written only then); linedash!, taking a list of dash
   lengths; and closepath!.  linemod! is now exported.

* Changes in behaviour

** graph's default output format is now "meta", as documented, instead
   of "X".

** Invalid settings, such as a zero tick spacing or an infinite or NaN
   axis limit, now raise a Scheme error.  A zero tick spacing used to end
   the whole Guile process, and infinite or NaN limits drew garbage.
   A nonpositive limit on a logarithmic axis is now an error too.

** graph's warnings now go to its #:errp port, not the process's
   standard error.

** #:pen-colors now applies to one graph only, rather than to every
   later graph too.  A malformed colors string is an error.

** Text written to a port before or between plotting calls now stays in
   order with the plotter's output on that port.

** On a logarithmic axis, a point whose error bar reaches zero or below
   is now kept, with the bar clipped at the axis, rather than dropped.

** Integer arguments accept integer-valued reals, such as 1.0.

** A tick spacing that would give too many ticks is ignored, with a
   warning, rather than overflowing.

** Importing (plotutils graph) no longer warns that merge overrides a
   core binding; it is declared as a replacement.

** Reading graph data takes linear time: large data sets are much
   faster (20,000 points took 2.6 s, now about 3 ms).

* Bug fixes

** Fixed double frees when a data list contains a non-number, and when
   a label or other string can't be converted to the locale's encoding.

** Fixed file descriptor and memory leaks in newpl and graph.

** graph's #:pen-colors option always raised an error; #:symbol N was
   ignored; #:rotation, #:title-font-size, and #:blankout were silently
   ignored.

** A function graphed over a range now includes the upper end of the
   range.

** #:x-limits and #:y-limits can now give an upper limit or spacing
   without a lower limit, using #f, e.g. #:x-limits '(#f 10).

** Linemodes -1 to -5 now use all five colors; cyan was never used.

* Documentation

** New manual entries for all of the above, and corrections to many
   entries and examples.

** The gallery images are regenerated.  The Postscript versions of the
   plot examples were empty, and the Gaussian histogram, golden spiral,
   and Koch snowflake examples drew wrongly; all are fixed.  A new
   example shows error bars.

* Library

** The extension library's libtool version is now 3:0:0, so its soname
   is libguile-plotutils.so.3.  The Scheme modules load it themselves;
   only programs linking to it directly are affected.

Changes in 1.0.3 (since 1.0.2)
* Fixed 'make dist' and 'make distcheck', which failed because
  removed helper scripts were still listed in EXTRA_DIST.

Changes in 1.0.2 (since 1.0.1)
* Fixed bug where toggle-auto-bump! was actually transposing axes
* Fixed bug where title-font-size! failed to change the font size
* Fixed various memory leaks.
* Changed default install directories to those reported by Guile
  macros during configure by default.
* Adds the configure option '--with-gnu-filesystem-hierarchy'
  to install in directories under $datadir and $libdir instead.
  This configure option is used by default under 'make distcheck'